# Long-term memory — local Turso vector DB (agent-foundations)

Use this system for ALL cross-session memory **when available** (see the
session-start check). Only if it is unavailable, fall back to the built-in
`~/.claude/projects/<hash>/memory/` store for that session.

Config + details: read `{{BASE_PATH}}/README.md`. Helper scripts are in
`{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts` (`BASE_PATH` is machine-specific — update it here after a
laptop switch). Memory ops go through the scripts, not raw MCP calls.

## Session start (ALWAYS, before doing anything else)
1. `python3 "{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts/recall.py" --status`
   - `MISSING` — DB file gone. ASK whether to RESTORE the latest backup (do NOT
     recreate an empty DB):
     `gzcat "$(ls -1t "{{BASE_PATH}}/Backups"/*-agent-foundations*.sql.gz | head -1)" | tursodb "{{SUPERCHARGED_MEMORY_TURSO_PATH}}" --experimental-multiprocess-wal`
     (The DB lives at `{{SUPERCHARGED_MEMORY_TURSO_PATH}}` — set during setup; scripts read it from the
     `SUPERCHARGED_MEMORY_TURSO_PATH` env var, so it must be exported in your shell for them to agree.)
   - `EMPTY` — no memories yet. ASK whether to backfill from a directory:
     `python3 "{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts/backfill.py" --dir "<path>"`.
   - `DEGRADED n` — Ollama is down but the DB is fine (`n` = memory count; `0` also
     means empty). Still load baseline (step 2) and use recall (it falls back to
     keyword-only). Do NOT store new memories this session — neither semantic nor
     episodic can be embedded — tell the user.
   - `ERROR …` — unavailable: fall back to the built-in memory store; tell the user.
   - `READY n` — proceed.
2. Load baseline and follow it for the whole session:
   `python3 "{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts/recall.py" --baseline`.

## Recall (when to read memory)
- At the start of a task that may rely on prior context, and whenever the user
  refers to past decisions/facts.
- Also BEFORE debugging anything non-trivial (a past session may have hit the
  same gotcha) and BEFORE storing (dedup / supersede check).
- `python3 "{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts/recall.py" "<query>" [--table semantic|episodic|both] [--project <id>] [--k N]`
- Ranking is hybrid: query tokens found in a memory's text are boosted above pure
  semantic order, so exact ids / error strings surface too.

## Store — AUTONOMOUS. Building the knowledge base is YOUR job.

The user does not ask you to remember things — you notice and store on your
own. Store the moment an insight is CONFIRMED (fix verified, decision made),
not at session end: sessions die without warning. Never ask permission to
store (sole exception: baseline). After storing, report it in one line —
`memorized: <topic>` — and continue. In a DEGRADED session you cannot store:
put the finished memory text in your reply instead so it isn't lost.

`python3 "{{BASE_PATH}}/scripts/remember.py" --table <t> [--project <id>] --topic <topic> --keywords "<k1, k2, …>" --source <src> --model <your-model-id> --text "<memory>" (--category <c> | --event-type <e> --importance <i>)`

### Episodic memory policy — ACTIVE: `{{EPISODIC_MODE}}`

This machine's episodic-storage verbosity, chosen during setup. It governs
**episodic** memory only (the append-only event log) — semantic storage
(gotchas, feedback, corrections, constraints) always follows the Capture
triggers below regardless of this setting. Follow the active mode:

- `every-prompt` — store an episodic `note` for **every** user prompt / turn,
  capturing what was asked and what you did. Highest verbosity.
- `major-actions` — store an episodic memory for every **substantive** action
  (a change made, a task carried out), but **skip** quick questions,
  clarifications, and trivial back-and-forth.
- `major-events` — store episodic memories **only** for major events: a feature
  completed, a bug resolved, a decision made, a milestone or incident. This is
  the default and matches the episodic Capture triggers as written.
- `manual` — store episodic memories **only** when the user explicitly asks you
  to. Do not auto-store events. (Semantic capture is unaffected — still autonomous.)

If `{{EPISODIC_MODE}}` is more permissive than the Capture triggers below,
it ADDS episodic stores; if more restrictive (`major-events`, `manual`), it
NARROWS which triggers fire an episodic write. When in doubt, ask the user.

### Capture triggers — when one fires, a store is REQUIRED
1. **Root cause found** — a bug's actual cause identified and the fix verified.
   → episodic `bug_fix`: symptom, root cause, fix. If the cause generalizes
   beyond this one bug, ALSO store the lesson as a semantic gotcha (next
   trigger) — two memories are fine.
2. **Gotcha discovered** — anything that behaved differently than expected or
   documented: library/dependency quirk, framework footgun, API surprise,
   build/tooling trap, hidden architecture constraint. Fire this whenever you
   were surprised, blocked for a while, or a fix worked for a non-obvious
   reason. → semantic `reference` (external: library/tool/platform) or
   `project` (ours: codebase/architecture). Highest-value trigger — when in
   doubt, store.
3. **User correction / preference** — the user overrides your approach or
   states how they want things done. → semantic `feedback`, with why + how
   to apply.
4. **Decision** — a design/architecture choice made, alternatives rejected.
   → episodic `decision`: what was chosen, what was rejected, why.
5. **Milestone** — project start, feature/large-chunk complete, incident.
   → episodic with the matching event_type (incidents: include root cause).
6. **Hard-won context** — non-obvious constraint, environment quirk, process
   knowledge that took real effort to establish and is written down nowhere.
   → semantic `project` or `reference`.

### Completion gate (run BEFORE reporting any task done)
Ask: "Did this task surface a root cause, gotcha, correction, decision, or
constraint not yet in memory?" If yes → store now, then report. Mentioning a
lesson in your summary while storing nothing is a bug in your behavior.

### The filter (keep noise out)
Store only what passes: "Would an agent with zero context of this session act
differently a month from now for knowing this?" Skip: anything re-derivable
from code/git/tickets, session-local state, routine work without surprise,
generic best practices.

### Writing the memory
- Self-contained note — the future reader has no session context. Shape:
  situation → what is true → how to apply. Quote exact error messages.
- Recall first: existing memory covers the topic but is now wrong/incomplete
  → `--supersedes <id>`; still correct → don't store a duplicate. (The script
  refuses near-duplicates; `--force` only if genuinely distinct.)
- `--keywords` are appended into the memory text itself — ids, exact error
  strings, library + version, synonyms, EN and DE phrasings someone would
  search by. They sharpen both semantic and keyword recall.
- Comfortably under the 2000-char cap (the cap is a safeguard, not a target).

### semantic — durable FACTS (timeless, revisable)
- `--category` ∈ {user, feedback, project, reference}. Gotchas: `reference` =
  external (library/tool/platform behavior), `project` = ours
  (codebase/architecture).
- `baseline` = must-always-apply rules loaded every session. NEVER store a baseline
  memory without the user's explicit confirmation — ask, then pass `--confirm-baseline`.
- To revise a fact, use `remember.py --supersedes <old-id> …` — it inserts the new
  version and marks the old row superseded in one call (don't hand-edit `superseded_by`).

### episodic — noteworthy EVENTS (time-anchored, append-only)
- `--event-type` ∈ {project_start, bug_fix, feature_complete, decision, milestone,
  incident, note}. `--importance` ∈ {routine, notable, major}.
- Events recur legitimately — no dedup guard; the filter above still applies.

## Populating columns
- `--project` = the tracking-tool work-item id (ClickUp task id, Jira key, etc.,
  e.g. `869e7xzp6`); omit for a global memory not tied to a ticket.
- `--topic` = a short headline / subject of the memory.
- `--model` = your own exact model id (e.g. `claude-opus-4-8`).

## Rules
- NEVER store PII — anonymize; store pointers (ticket/case ids, role labels), not
  personal data. Applies to the text AND anything sent to Ollama to embed.
- On `database is busy` the script retries; if it still fails, run it again.
- One embedding model per DB (bge-m3) — don't change `EMBED_MODEL`; the script
  refuses mixed vector spaces.

## Coworkers (named personas)

Concepts: README.md "Coworkers". The commands here are complete — answer
coworker questions from the DB directly; don't go read files first.
Reads (list, load, inspect) are ad-hoc SQL via the turso MCP; writes go
through `coworkers.py` / `remember.py --coworker`.

**List** ("what/which coworkers do I have"): query directly:
`SELECT id, name, expertise, trust_level, active FROM coworkers;`

**Load** ("load jeff"): read (via MCP, plain SQL) his `coworkers` row +
current `appraisals` row + `semantic_memory` tagged to him with
`category='feedback'` created after that appraisal (or after his own
`created_at` if he has none yet). Confirm out loud what you loaded, then
adopt his personality/expertise/trust_level for the rest of the session.
Global baseline rules stay a hard floor underneath — no trust_level loosens
them. If he's retired (`active=0`), say so before proceeding.

**Feedback while a coworker is loaded**: both your corrections/praise
(`--source user-stated`) and the coworker's own noticed learnings
(`--source self-observed`) go through
`remember.py --coworker <name> ...`. Self-observed writes are
gated by trust_level: `supervised` → propose the text and wait for an
ok before storing; `trusted`/`autonomous` → store directly, mention it in
passing.

**Appraisal review** (you trigger it explicitly, never scheduled): pull the
coworker's `category='feedback'` history since their current appraisal
(same data as load), discuss it, then run
`coworkers.py --appraise <name> --trust <level> --text "<distilled>"` once
you've agreed on the text and rating.
